vmware player is a free download, so why not download it and see if it
does what you want? It doesn't let you create new virtual machines, but
you can download lots of prebuilt ones, or even create new custom images
at www.easyvmx.com (and then you install the os into the image from a cd
or iso file). VMWare workstation can be downloaded for free evaluation
(and after the evaluation period expires you can continue running any
virtual machine you create using vmware player). 

The OS images usually live inside files, so you don't boot from an
existing partition at all: if you want to boot Debian you just download
a Debian image, put the file anywhere convenient, and boot from that.
However, with VMWare workstation you can create an image which boots
from an existing partition: 

http://www.vmware.com/support/reference/linux/rawdevices_linux.html:

>  It is possible to install VMware Workstation for Linux to run one or
>  more guest operating systems from raw disk partitions. This is useful
>  for people who already have a dual- or multiboot system and who want
>  to run those operating systems under VMware Workstation. If you don't
>  have a dual-boot system but want to install a guest operating system
>  into an unused partition through a virtual machine please refer to
>  this tech note. For some applications raw disk may also provide
>  better disk I/O performance than using a virtual disk. Just as with
>  virtual disks, VMware Workstation enables a raw disk to be used in
>  persistent, undoable, and nonpersistent modes; see a detailed
>  explanation of these modes. 
> 
> Caution: Raw disk support is an advanced feature of VMware Workstation
> and should be enabled by users who are already familiar with the
> product. To familiarize yourself you should, at minimum, create and
> configure a virtual machine with a virtual disk and install an
> operating system. In addition, booting a previously installed
> operating system within a virtual machine may not work on some
> existing installations. Your experience may vary depending on the your
> hardware configuration and guest operating system installation. 


